WEBVTT

00:00:01.250 --> 00:00:03.240
A turn of the handle looks simple.

00:00:03.650 --> 00:00:04.970
Inside Home Soda Machine,

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:06.670
three things make it possible:

00:00:06.710 --> 00:00:09.200
pressure, mixing, and cold.

00:00:10.930 --> 00:00:11.850
Pull away the enclosure,

00:00:11.890 --> 00:00:13.720
and this is the cold core.

00:00:13.970 --> 00:00:16.110
At its center is a
stainless steel carbonator:

00:00:16.150 --> 00:00:17.630
a sealed tank that keeps water

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:19.470
in contact with carbon dioxide.

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:23.900
Carbon dioxide enters
through a port at the bottom.

00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:25.170
While gas is entering,

00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:26.170
bubbles rise through the water

00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:28.010
and collect in the space above it.

00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:30.720
As the pressures balance,
gas admission stops.

00:00:32.620 --> 00:00:34.530
Fresh water comes in from the top.

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:35.760
Look closely at the inlet:

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:38.460
a tiny, one-sixteenth-inch hole

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:40.910
turns the incoming flow into a narrow jet.

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:44.410
The stream crosses the
carbon dioxide headspace

00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:46.360
and plunges into the water below.

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:48.410
Its impact draws gas into the pool

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:50.250
and drives circulation.

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:52.550
Those moving interfaces
give carbon dioxide

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:54.480
more opportunities to dissolve.

00:00:56.610 --> 00:00:58.420
The bubbles are the process you can see.

00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:00.040
The carbonation you drink

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:01.880
is gas dissolved in the water.

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:06.440
As the level rises,
a magnetic float follows it.

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:08.940
At the high-level signal,
the refill pump stops.

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:12.820
Now look outside the tank.

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:14.890
This copper coil carries refrigerant.

00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:17.010
As it evaporates, it absorbs heat

00:01:17.050 --> 00:01:19.200
through the coil
and the stainless steel wall.

00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:22.500
The refrigeration loop
carries that heat away.

00:01:24.190 --> 00:01:26.560
Cold water can hold
more dissolved carbon dioxide.

00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:30.560
Pressure, mixing, and
cooling work together,

00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:32.950
so the next pour starts with
chilled, carbonated water,

00:01:32.990 --> 00:01:36.000
ready for a separate stream
of flavor at the faucet.

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:38.840
Home Soda Machine.

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:40.680
Cold soda, on tap.
